msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:170
msgid "where $m(t)$ is [`activity_multiplier`](#activity_multiplier). It equals `(b.dependents + b.recent_dependents + b.downloads) * b.multiplier` for `b = score_breakdown(signals)`, bit for bit."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:174
msgid "The score is finite and non-negative for every input. It is `0` exactly when all five counts are `0` or negative, and it never decreases when a count grows or the release becomes more recent. It has no fixed upper limit; with every count at the largest `Int` it is about `2110`. Scores are comparable across packages because each depends only on that package's signals; the rank and the momentum label depend on the population."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:206
msgid "`score_breakdown` returns the three weighted terms of the base score and the multiplier, so that a reader can see where a score comes from."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:213
msgid "The fields of the result are"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:215
#: manual/design/static_search.md:46
msgid "Value"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:222
msgid "with the notation of [`compute_score`](#compute_score). Each term is non-negative and the multiplier lies in $[0.88, 1.12]$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:254
msgid "`ScoreBreakdown` is the result of `score_breakdown`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:265
msgid "The fields are read-only outside the package; you obtain a value from `score_breakdown` or from the `breakdown` field of a [`ScoreSnapshot`](#scoresnapshot). `ToJson` and `FromJson` use the field names as keys. The struct does not implement `Eq`; compare the fields or the JSON forms."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:295
msgid "`DEPENDENTS_WEIGHT` is the weight of the dependents term of the base score."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:301
msgid "A term is its weight times the logarithmic signal, so the weight is the score gained, before the multiplier, each time the signal plus one grows by the factor $e$. The dependents term has the largest weight of the three."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:323
msgid "`RECENT_DEPENDENTS_WEIGHT` is the weight of the recent-dependents term of the base score."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:330
msgid "A recent dependent also counts in the dependents term, so it adds to both terms."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:351
msgid "`DOWNLOADS_WEIGHT` is the weight of the downloads term of the base score."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:357
msgid "It is the smallest of the three weights. Downloads count only through their logarithm: 999 downloads give $22 \\ln 1000 \\approx 152$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:376
msgid "Score terms"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:380
msgid "`weighted_dependents` returns the dependent count that enters the score: external dependents count once, dependents of the same owner count `SELF_DEPENDENT_WEIGHT`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:388
msgid "The arguments are the external and the same-owner count, in this order. Each is clamped to `0` first. The result is exact, because a quarter of an `Int` is representable as a `Double`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:402
msgid "`SELF_DEPENDENT_WEIGHT` is the weight of a dependent published by the same owner as the package it depends on."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:409
msgid "An owner who splits one project into many packages adds one dependent per package. Counting each of them fully would let a package rank on its author's own reuse, so four same-owner dependents weigh as much as one external dependent."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:432
msgid "`log_signal` returns $\\sigma(x) = \\ln(1 + x)$ for $x > 0$ and `0` otherwise."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:438
msgid "Zero, negative values, `-Infinity` and `NaN` all give `0`; `+Infinity` gives `+Infinity`. The function uses [`ln`](#ln), so it returns the same bits on every backend."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:454
msgid "`ln` returns the natural logarithm of `x`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:460
msgid "The function is a port of the fdlibm `e_log.c` routine built only from IEEE 754 basic operations and bit manipulation. Its error is below one unit in the last place, and, unlike `@math.ln`, which calls the platform's `Math.log` on the JavaScript backend, it returns the same bits on every backend, so scores and ranks do not depend on the backend. The special values follow IEEE 754:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:469
msgid "`+0.0` or `-0.0`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:470
msgid "negative, including `-Infinity`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:473
msgid "subnormal"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:473
msgid "finite, for example `ln(5.0e-324)` is about `-744.44`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:488
msgid "`activity_multiplier` returns the release-recency factor $m(t)$ that scales the whole base score."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:503
msgid "A negative `days_since_release` counts as `0`. The function is continuous and non-increasing; it is `1.0` near 197.5 days, halfway through the slope."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:517
msgid "Ranks"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:521
msgid "`rank_positions` returns the competition rank of every score, best first."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:527
msgid "The position of a score is one plus the number of strictly greater scores, so equal scores share a position and the next position is skipped (\"1, 2, 2, 4\"). The result has the same length and order as the input. `NaN` counts as smaller than every number, and all `NaN` values share the last position. `0.0` and `-0.0` are equal. An empty view gives an empty array."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:545
msgid "`rank_label` returns the rank label `S`, `A`, `B`, `C` or `D` of a package at competition position `position` (`1` is best) among `population` packages."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:552
msgid "The arguments are the position, the population and the package's score. With $q = (\\text{position} - 1) / \\text{population}$, the share of the population that scores strictly higher:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:556
#: manual/api/score.md:626
msgid "Label"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:564
msgid "A package without any signal is always `D`: the label is `D` when `score` is `0`, negative or `NaN`, whatever its position. It is also `D` when `population` or `position` is `0` or negative. Tied packages share a position and therefore a label. Use the positions from `rank_positions` over the same population; the function does not check that `position` is at most `population`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:585
msgid "`rank_labels` returns the rank labels, best first."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:591
msgid "The result is `[\"S\", \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", \"D\"]`, and every result of `rank_label` is one of its elements. Each call returns a new array, so changing it does not affect later calls. The query language and the search take the labels from here instead of repeating them."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:611
#: manual/design/score.md:141
msgid "Momentum"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:615
msgid "`compute_momentum_label` returns `New`, `Rising`, `Cooling` or `Stable` from a package's score now and 30 days ago."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:622
msgid "The second argument is `None` when the package had no release 30 days ago. With the change $G = S - S_{30}$ and the threshold $T = \\max(\\texttt{MOMENTUM\\_MIN\\_CHANGE},\\ \\texttt{MOMENTUM\\_MIN\\_RATIO} \\cdot S_{30})$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:628
msgid "`score_30d_ago` is `None`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:631
msgid "otherwise"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:633
msgid "The threshold is at least 10 points and at least 10 % of the earlier score, so a large package needs a large change and a small one cannot change its label with a handful of points. A `NaN` score or earlier score gives `Stable`, unless the earlier score is `None`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:652
msgid "`momentum_labels` returns the momentum labels."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:658
msgid "The result is `[\"New\", \"Rising\", \"Stable\", \"Cooling\"]`, and every result of `compute_momentum_label` is one of its elements. Each call returns a new array."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:679
msgid "`MOMENTUM_MIN_CHANGE` is the smallest change of the score, in points, that counts as momentum."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:686
msgid "It decides the threshold while the earlier score is below 100."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:699
msgid "`MOMENTUM_MIN_RATIO` is the smallest change of the score, as a share of its value 30 days ago, that counts as momentum."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:706
msgid "It decides the threshold from an earlier score of 100 up."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:717
msgid "Populations"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:721
msgid "`score_population` scores a whole population of packages and returns one `ScoreSnapshot` per package."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:728
msgid "`current[i]` holds the signals of package `i` now, and `past[i]` its signals 30 days ago, or `None` when the package had no release then. The result has the length and order of `current`. When `past` is shorter than `current`, the missing entries count as `None`; extra entries are ignored."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:733
msgid "The function computes every score with `compute_score`, the positions with `rank_positions` over all current scores, and for package `i`:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:736
msgid "`rank_label` is `rank_label(position, current.length(), score)`;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:737
msgid "`score_30d_ago` is the score of `past[i]`, or `0` for `None`;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:738
msgid "`momentum_label` is `compute_momentum_label(score, past score)`, so a package with `None` is `New`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/score.md:741
msgid "Positions and labels are relative to the population you pass: the same signals can be `S` in a small population and `C` in a large one. An empty population gives an empty array."
